---
import LearnLayout from "../../components/learn/LearnLayout.astro";
import LearnNav from "../../components/learn/LearnNav.astro";
import { tagBlob, tagTree, RELEASE_URL, RELEASE_CHANGELOG } from "../../data/learn";

const description =
  "PeaceMaker is hipfire's own kernel builder for RDNA: code objects written without a ROCm assembler, lifted back byte for byte, certified for waits, hazards and LDS bounds, and byte-identical to the hipcc kernels they replace in 0.4.1.";
---

<LearnLayout title="PeaceMaker: certified kernels" description={description}>
  <article class="distil">
    <div class="distil-meta"><a href="/learn">/learn</a> &middot; kernels &middot; certification</div>
    <h1 class="distil-title">PeaceMaker: certified kernels</h1>
    <p class="distil-lede">
      A compiler hands you a code object. It does not hand you a proof that every
      memory wait is in place, that no two waves race through shared memory, or
      that the hardware's instruction hazards are covered. On RDNA those are the
      programmer's problem, and the bugs they cause are intermittent.
    </p>
    <p class="distil-lede">
      <strong>PeaceMaker (PM)</strong> is hipfire's own kernel authoring and
      assembly path. It writes RDNA code objects itself, reads them back byte for
      byte, and checks them before they ship. In 0.4.1 several of Flash-Next's
      hottest prefill kernels run from PM-built modules by default, each one a
      byte-identical replacement for the hipcc kernel it displaces.
    </p>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">01</span> What can go wrong below the compiler</h2>
      <span class="lx-tag">the idea</span>
      <p>
        RDNA issues memory operations asynchronously. A load or an LDS store
        returns immediately and increments a counter; the program must wait on
        that counter (<code>s_waitcnt</code> on gfx11, <code>s_wait_dscnt</code>
        and friends on gfx12) before it reads the result, or before a barrier
        tells other waves the data is ready. Some instruction pairs also need a
        gap between them (<code>s_delay_alu</code>, <code>s_wait_alu</code>
        guards). Get one wrong and the kernel still runs, usually gives the right
        answer, and occasionally does not.
      </p>
      <div class="distil-fig">
        <div class="fig-caption">fig &middot; the bug class: an LDS store not drained before the barrier that publishes it</div>
        <pre class="ascii-fig">{`  wave A                         wave B
  ds_store  [slot 0] ← x          ·
  ·  (store still in flight)      ·
  s_barrier  ───────────────────  s_barrier
                                  ds_load  [slot 0]   ← may read the old value
  fix:  s_waitcnt lgkmcnt(0)  (gfx11) / s_wait_dscnt 0 (gfx12)  before s_barrier`}</pre>
      </div>
      <p>
        hipfire has hit exactly this class in production kernels: a Strix Halo
        verify-attention co-residency race and a gfx11 FA2 mailbox race. 0.4.1
        also adds explicit store waits before every LDS-only barrier in the
        Gated DeltaNet kernels.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">02</span> The PM pipeline</h2>
      <span class="lx-tag">how hipfire does it</span>
      <div class="distil-fig">
        <div class="fig-caption">fig &middot; from kernel source to a certified module</div>
        <div class="lx-flow">
          <div class="lx-flow-step"><b>author</b><span>kernel written in Rust, run at build time, through a typed core</span></div>
          <div class="lx-flow-step"><b>build</b><span>checked builder keeps a wait ledger, LDS slot states and hazard trackers per instruction</span></div>
          <div class="lx-flow-step"><b>emit</b><span>native code object v6, no ROCm assembler, linker or bundler</span></div>
          <div class="lx-flow-step"><b>lift</b><span>decode every word, re-encode, re-emit: must equal the input byte for byte</span></div>
          <div class="lx-flow-step ok"><b>certify</b><span>whole-program wait, hazard, barrier and LDS-bounds checks; any obligation fails</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <h3>Author: races as type errors</h3>
      <p>
        <code>peacemaker-author</code> puts types in front of the builder. Targets
        are types (<code>Gfx1100</code>, <code>Gfx1151</code>, <code>Gfx1201</code>);
        an LDS region carries its ownership state (<code>Free</code>,
        <code>Writing</code>, <code>Published</code>); a store returns a
        <code>Pending</code> token that must become <code>Drained</code> before the
        barrier that publishes it; a loop must carry the same state on entry and
        on its back edge. The two production races above are now
        <strong>rustc compile errors</strong>, pinned by compile-fail tests. The
        types emit nothing of their own, so kernels ported onto this core
        re-emit byte-identical assembly.
      </p>
      <h3>Build and emit: no ROCm in the path</h3>
      <p>
        The builder lowers each instruction through hipfire's own gfx11/gfx12
        encoding tables and writes the whole code object: <code>.text</code>,
        the 64-byte kernel descriptors, the MessagePack AMDGPU metadata note,
        symbol and dynamic tables, and the offload-bundle wrapper. The ROCm
        assembler/linker path survives only as a test oracle. Across every
        PM-emitted symbol on gfx1100, gfx1151 and gfx1201 (55 symbols, as 55
        single-symbol objects and 12 modules), every section other than the
        version-stamp comment is byte-identical to what <code>llvm-mc</code> +
        <code>ld.lld</code> + <code>clang-offload-bundler</code> produce.
      </p>
      <h3>Lift: the bytes are the proof</h3>
      <p>
        <code>peacemaker-lift</code> turns a code object back into a typed
        program: every instruction word is decoded through pinned opcode tables
        (unknown encodings are rejected), re-encoded from its typed fields and
        compared word for word, and the module is re-emitted and must equal the
        input byte for byte. Analyses then run on exactly the bytes that ship,
        not on a source that might compile differently.
      </p>
      <h3>Certify: <code>pm_check</code></h3>
      <ul class="distil-ul">
        <li><strong>wait replay</strong> over the gfx11 VM / LGKM / VS counters (and the gfx12 equivalents), across the control-flow graph and loop back-edges;</li>
        <li><strong>hazard replay</strong>: gfx1100 transcendental use, VCMPX&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;PERMLANE, WMMA chaining, joined over back-edges;</li>
        <li><strong>barrier pairing</strong> and the rule that every LDS store is drained before a barrier;</li>
        <li><code>s_clause</code> / <code>s_delay_alu</code> windows and register resources;</li>
        <li><strong><code>lds_bounds</code></strong>: every LDS address is evaluated for every lane of every wave of the workgroup, and every access must end inside the launch allocation.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Any open obligation fails certification. The shipped QSA and MQ6 modules certify with zero obligations.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">03</span> Twins: same math, better schedule</h2>
      <span class="lx-tag">how hipfire does it</span>
      <p>
        A PM kernel that replaces a hipcc kernel is written as an
        <strong>exact twin</strong>: the same unpack, the same dequantization
        instructions, the same WMMA lane map and the same ascending accumulation
        chain per output. Only tiling and scheduling change. The gfx1201 MQ6
        trunk GEMM is a good example:
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th></th><th>hipcc kernel</th><th>PM twin</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>work per wave</td><td>one dequant feeds 8 WMMAs</td><td>a wave owns 16 rows &times; 256 tokens; one dequant feeds 16 WMMAs</td></tr>
            <tr><td>activation staging</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>double-buffered 32-K LDS chunks, one barrier per chunk</td></tr>
            <tr><td>B fragments</td><td>wait on LDS before each WMMA</td><td>8-deep register ring, no per-WMMA <code>s_wait_dscnt 0</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>arithmetic</td><td colspan="2">identical: 6-bit unpack, <code>v_cvt_f32_ubyte0</code>, <code>v_cvt_f16_f32</code>, one <code>v_fma_f16</code>, ascending 16-K chain, <code>0 + acc</code> F32 epilogue</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Twins are checked by <code>memcmp</code> against the hipcc object, not
        by tolerance. For that MQ6 kernel: zero differing bytes over the six
        pp8192 trace shapes and 216 further runs over 12 shapes, including
        ragged tails and inputs that produce infinities and NaNs. For the
        Strix Halo QSA selector: every score bit, the whole ordered selection
        and its fill byte-identical across 83 cases and 707 records, including
        real 32K/64K captures of all 12 QSA layers.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">04</span> Checking the kernels PM didn't write</h2>
      <p>
        Because the lifter reads any gfx11/gfx12 code object, the same analyses
        run over hipcc output. <code>peacemaker audit --lds-barrier</code> flags
        any path, loop back-edges included, that reaches a workgroup barrier
        with an LDS store still pending: the bug class from &sect;&nbsp;01. Over
        the gfx1201 JIT caches (412 modules, 62 distinct objects) it lifted
        every module byte-exactly and reported 0 findings. A module the tables
        cannot fully decode is reported as <em>unchecked</em>, never as clean.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">05</span> What ships PM-built in 0.4.1</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>kernel</th><th>arch</th><th>route</th><th>opt-out</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>QSA gathered attention</strong> (convert + attention)</td><td><code>gfx1151</code>, <code>gfx1201</code></td><td>default, byte-exact replacement</td><td><code>HIPFIRE_QWEN4_QSA_PM=0</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>QSA selector</strong>: rows16 score + select-from-scores</td><td><code>gfx1151</code></td><td>default for prefill calls of &ge; 512 rows; byte-exact</td><td><code>HIPFIRE_QWEN4_QSA_SCORE_PM=0</code>, <code>…_SELECT_PM=0</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>QSA selector score</strong> for the opt-in q8 state</td><td><code>gfx1151</code></td><td>default when q8 QSA is chosen; byte-exact</td><td><code>HIPFIRE_QWEN4_QSA_SCORE_PM=0</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>MQ6 trunk GEMM</strong></td><td><code>gfx1201</code>; large shapes on <code>gfx1151</code></td><td>default, byte-exact replacement</td><td><code>HIPFIRE_QWEN4_MQ6_X4_PM=0</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Symmetric IU4 MoE</strong> grouped expert GEMMs</td><td><code>gfx1151</code>, <code>gfx1201</code></td><td>default for symmetric artifacts (the default Flash-Next pull); a new numeric route, KLD-gated</td><td><code>HIPFIRE_QWEN4_MOE_SYM_IU4=0</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>27B dense IU4 prefill</strong>, fused gate/up&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;A4 epilogue</td><td><code>gfx1151</code></td><td>default on admitted shapes, byte-exact</td><td><code>HIPFIRE_V2B_A4_EPI=0</code></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="footnote">
        Graph capture and recorded replays, decode and verify, and unsupported
        shapes keep the hipcc kernels where the table says &ldquo;prefill&rdquo;.
        The certified modules are the <code>*.hxaco</code> files in
        <a href={tagTree("kernels")}><code>kernels/</code></a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">06</span> What it buys you</h2>
      <div class="lx-buys">
        <div class="lx-buys-head">0.4.1 &middot; from the release notes and changelog</div>
        <div class="lx-stats">
          <div class="lx-stat">
            <div class="lx-stat-val accent">0<span class="unit">bytes</span></div>
            <div class="lx-stat-label">difference vs the hipcc kernels for the PM routes on by default</div>
            <div class="lx-stat-sub">PM QSA gather, PM QSA score/select, PM MQ6 trunk are listed as byte-exact replacements</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lx-stat">
            <div class="lx-stat-val">0<span class="unit">ROCm tools</span></div>
            <div class="lx-stat-label">in the PM build path</div>
            <div class="lx-stat-sub">assembler, linker and bundler replaced by the native writer; ROCm stays as a test oracle</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lx-stat">
            <div class="lx-stat-val">2<span class="unit">races</span></div>
            <div class="lx-stat-label">real LDS races from hipfire's kernels that are now compile errors</div>
            <div class="lx-stat-sub">Halo verify-attention <code>lgkmcnt</code> race; gfx11 FA2 mailbox race</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="lx-buys-foot">
          PM routes are part of the Flash-Next prefill that 0.4.1 ships in its
          kernel packs for Strix Halo and the R9700, so those workloads run
          without cold-cache device compilation. Because the QSA and MQ6
          replacements are byte-exact, turning one off changes speed, never output.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">07</span> Limits</h2>
      <div class="lx-limit">
        <div class="lx-limit-title">scope</div>
        <ul class="distil-ul">
          <li>PM targets gfx1100, gfx1151 and gfx1201. Other architectures run hipcc kernels.</li>
          <li>Byte-identity is checked per kernel against its hipcc twin; the symmetric IU4 MoE route is a different numeric route from the F16 one it replaces and is KLD-gated, not bit-exact.</li>
          <li>The audit covers what the decode tables cover; anything else is reported unchecked.</li>
          <li>The XDNA2 NPU work that shares the PM name space is experimental and outside inference.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="distil-section">
      <h2><span class="num">08</span> Sources</h2>
      <ul class="distil-ul">
        <li><a href={tagTree("crates/hipfire-isa")}><code>crates/hipfire-isa</code></a>: the checked builder, native writer and <code>pm_check</code> (<a href={tagBlob("crates/hipfire-isa/src/pm_check.rs")}><code>src/pm_check.rs</code></a>).</li>
        <li><a href={tagTree("crates/peacemaker-author")}><code>crates/peacemaker-author</code></a>: typed kernel language M0.</li>
        <li><a href={tagTree("crates/peacemaker-lift")}><code>crates/peacemaker-lift</code></a> and <a href={tagTree("crates/peacemaker-ir")}><code>crates/peacemaker-ir</code></a>: lifter, codecs and analyses.</li>
        <li><a href={RELEASE_CHANGELOG}>CHANGELOG, v0.4.1</a> (&ldquo;PM / kernel toolchain&rdquo; and the Flash-Next PM entries) and the <a href={RELEASE_URL}>v0.4.1 release notes</a> (exact-replacement defaults).</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <LearnNav current="/learn/peacemaker" />
  </article>
</LearnLayout>
